CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member template-id after non-dependent class-template-id \(true case\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ member template-id after non-dependent class-template-id \(false case\): SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
N5008 [temp.names]/5.  The `template` keyword before a member template-id is
only required for a dependent nested-name-specifier; for a non-dependent
class-template-id such as `C<int>` it is optional.  CBMC's expression name
parser (rVarNameCore) previously rejected `C<int>::al<char>::value` (parse error
before '> :: value') while accepting the `template`-keyword form.  Two causes,
both fixed: (1) the speculative template-argument check did not accept a
following `::` (a `name<...>::` nested-name-specifier can only be a template-id),
and (2) it treated every template-id qualifier as dependent, whereas a concrete
instantiation like `C<int>` is not dependent (now mirrors rName by checking
whether the qualifier's arguments involve a template parameter).  Fires for a
member alias template and a nested class template alike, and needs only a single
inner argument.  This is the shape of libstdc++'s
`_TupleConstraints<_Types...>::__constructible<_UTypes...>` (written WITH the
keyword there, so std::tuple does not hit it).
